{"name":"Sapphire Lace","hex":"#235c8e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sapphire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A clear deep blue variety of corundum, valued as a precious stone.","A white, yellow, or purple variety of corundum, either clear or translucent."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["of a deep blue colour.","pertaining to a 45th year"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sapphire"},{"term":"lace","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A light fabric containing patterns of holes, usually built up from a single thread.","A cord or ribbon passed through eyelets in a shoe or garment, pulled tight and tied to fasten the shoe or garment firmly."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fasten (something) with laces.","To interweave items."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lace"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sapphire","blocks":[{"text":"Sapphire is a gemstone variety of the mineral corundum, the crystalline form of aluminium oxide (α-Al2O3). Pure corundum is colorless, while trace elements in its crystal structure produce a wide range of colors. Blue is the best-known, but sapphires also occur in yellow, green, purple, pink, orange, gray, black, and colorless forms. Red gem-quality corundum is classified as ruby rather than sapphire. The boundary between ruby and pink sapphire is based on color appearance and varies among gemological and trade conventions. Sapphire has a Mohs hardness of 9, making it highly resistant to scratching and abrasion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png/330px-Logan_Sapphire_10956420_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapphire"},{"title":"Lace","blocks":[{"text":"Lace is a decorative openwork textile made without the use of pre-existing fabric. Lace can be made by hand or machine. Various techniques can be employed to form lace, each having a distinct category. Lace can also be used to refer to other types of openwork fabric such as cutwork or filet, which use pre-existing fabric."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG/330px-Verknipte_kantstrook.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lace"},{"title":"Shades of blue","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color blue may differ in hue, chroma, or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a blue or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Shades_of_Blue.svg/330px-Shades_of_Blue.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/1280px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg/500px-Alkaline_pegmatite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue corundum crystals in weathered feldspar in an alkaline pegmatite from the Canaã massif, Brazil.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlkaline_pegmatite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg/500px-Sapphire_%28GeoDIL_number_-_1432%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedi